	abstract = {In the contemporary business environment, companies face numerous complex challenges: the distances between producers and customers are increasing, the environmental impact of companies actions must be carefully considered, and there is a simultaneous need to control costs while ensuring service quality. For companies that produce perishable goods, the situation is even more complex: they must ensure not only speedy service and minimal environmental impact but also guarantee that the delivered product meets specific quality standards to avoid customer rejection. The consequence of delivering unsuitable products is twofold: it results in lost sales opportunities and incurs additional disposal costs. In response to these challenges, we propose a mathematical model designed to optimize the delivery routes for a set of customers, considering both operational costs and environmental impact. At the same time, the proposed model ensures the delivery of products that meet a certain quality level, avoiding service when a minimum quality threshold cannot be satisfied. To evaluate the model's effectiveness, we present a case study focused on a company shipping Extra-Virgin Olive Oil (EVOO). This study examines the impact of transport temperature and travel times on product quality, providing valuable insights into enhancing operational efficiency while maintaining product integrity. © 2024 The Authors. Published by Elsevier B.V.},
